  "query_preview": "period of rain chances for late-October.\n\nFor Wednesday, deeper moisture will remain fixed over Southwest \nNew Mexico and Arizona. Isolated (10-20% coverage) showers and \nthunderstorms are expected over portions of Grant and Hidalgo \nCounties, but the majority of convective activity on Wednesday \nwill be limited further west into Arizona. Locations east of the \nContinental Divide will stay dry. Wednesday afternoon may also be \nthe warmest day of the week, with lowland highs warming further to\nthe lower-to-mid 80s. This is 5 to 10 degrees above average for \nlate October.\n\nForecast remains on track for widespread rain chances Thursday \nthrough Sunday across Southern New Mexico and Far West Texas. \nPacific low will move ashore late on Thursday, with a 75-knot jet \nstreak rounding the base of the system over the Southwest US. Deep\nmoisture flux out of Mexico will push precipitable water values \nto 1.1-1.3\", well above normal for this time of year. Widespread \nshowers and thunderstorms developing across E AZ and W NM on \nThursday will slowly shift its focus eastward across the Rio \nGrande Valley on Friday, then Far West Texas/Southeast New Mexico \nover the weekend. Storms will be capable of heavy rain and flash \nflooding. Current guidance put the highest flood risk over W NM, \nincluding Grant County and the Gila Wilderness (most likely rain \namounts 0.75-1.25\", worse-case 2.00-3.00\" localized). \n\nContinued jet flow around the base of the low will slow it down \nconsiderably this weekend, likely stalling a portion of vorticity \naloft over Arizona and maintaining scattered showers and \nthunderstorms across Eastern New Mexico and West Texas this \nweekend. Best rain chances over the weekend will be along and east\nof the Rio Grande, with localized flash flooding the main risk.",
